  "sections": [
    {
      "sectionId": "section-introduction",
      "heading": "Introduction",
      "sourceAnchor": "introduction",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Introduced in Ghosts of Saltmarsh and made playable by "
            },
            {
              "type": "link",
              "text": "Locathah Rising",
              "href": "https://www.dmsguild.com/product/289261/Locathah-Rising-5e?affiliate_id=389627"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " ("
            },
            {
              "type": "link",
              "text": "affiliate link",
              "href": "https://rpgbot.net/about/faq#what-are-affiliate-links-and-why-do-you-use-them"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "). Locathah Rising offers almost no detail on the Locathah beyond their traits, describing them as “proud fish folk”. The official art depicts them as somewhat goofy fish-like humanoids with big webbed hands and feet, a comically wide mouth, and whisker-like things similar to a catfish."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Mechanically, the Locathah is uncomplicated, but solidly designed, versatile,\n\tand well-balanced. Strength and Dexterity increases on the same race are rare\n\t(only the Bugbear and the Locathah have increases to both), you get some\n\tnatural armor, two good skills, and Advantage on saves against 6 status\n\teffects. Limited Amphibiousness makes the Locathah great for an aquatic\n\tcampaign, but if you have a big enough barrel you can still manage overland\n\ttravel. The Locathah doesn’t have any activated abilities to manage, so\n\tdespite their unique combination of traits they’re very easy to play."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-3",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "With the custom origin rules in place, the Locathah becomes even more\n\tversatile. They get the typical +2/+1 increases, two skills, natural armor,\n\tand the resistances provided by Leviathan Will. That makes them a broadly\n\tresilient basis for nearly any build. Without any active abilities, the\n\tLocathah is low-complexity and easy to play, but doesn’t add any additional\n\tactive options except skills."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-4",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "DnDBeyond denotes the Locathah as “Noncore D&D Material”, which means\n\tthat they’re only semi-official so you shouldn’t assume that you can use them\n\tin your campaign without your DM’s permission. I consider myself an “amateur\n\texpert” on race design in 5e, and personally I would allow the Locathah in my\n\tcampaigns. They’re roughly as strong as most of the races in the Player’s\n\tHandbook and they don’t have any traits that could turn into abuse cases.\n\tThey’re also allowed in Adventurer’s League, so even WotC seems to be happy\n\twith the Locathah’s design."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
